<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puebla (state)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puebla is a state of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, with its capital at the city of Puebla. Its highlands, bordered by the volcanoes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl">Popocatépetl</a> and Pico de Orizaba, were home to major Mesoamerican centres such as Cholula and Cantona. The city of Puebla, founded by the Spanish in 1531, is famous for its Talavera pottery and for the Mexican victory over the French on 5 May 1862.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puebla-state.jpg" alt="Puebla (state)" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakub Hejtmánek · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mexico-Popocatepetl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army that defeated Maximilian was a coalition of liberal forces under regional commanders such as Porfirio Díaz, who recaptured <b>Puebla</b> in April 1867, and Mariano Escobedo, the victor at Querétaro.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Culturally, New Spain produced a distinctive baroque civilisation: the cathedrals of Mexico City and <b>Puebla</b>, the poet and scholar Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz, the polymath Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora, and the Royal University of Mexico, founded in 1551.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Potters produced cylindrical tripod vessels, while Thin Orange ware, made in southern <b>Puebla</b>, was distributed through the city’s networks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Cantona grew from about 600 BCE on a rough lava field in eastern <b>Puebla</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cantona">Cantona</a></li></ul></section>
